virglrenderer: a lost Venus context counts in the status file, with its app's name

What: when vkr's ring thread ends a Venus context for good (vkr_context_on_ring_fatal,
"venus context N (NAME) is lost"), the context now counts where OmacVM.app and omacvm
check look for lost GPU contexts: the status file (OMACVM_GPU_MEMORY_STATUS) gets lost
+1, lost_last and lost_recent with its name, lost_recent_why "venus" ("guard" when the
budget had refused a Venus allocation of that context: with the guest's asynchronous
allocation the app's next use of that memory ends the context), and a new list
lost_recent_kind (gl or venus for each of lost_recent). The ring thread leaves the name
and cause in the shared budget module (src/virgl_gpu_budget.h,
virgl_gpu_budget_venus_lost: the newest eight keep their names, all are counted);
vrend's status writer takes them on the renderer thread at its next poll
(vrend_memory_tick) and writes the file at once, as for a lost GL context.
A context the guest was closing does not count. When an app's process ends, the guest's
kernel closes every handle of it before it destroys the context; a handle vkr still uses
(the ring's own buffer, a command stream being read, a reply being written) then ends
the context (vkr_context_destroy_resource), and the ring command that fails after it is
a teardown (SIGKILL during Zink's teardown on an M1 Max printed "is lost" for it).
vkr_context_destroy_resource now marks the context closing before it sets the fatal
flag; the ring-fatal path then logs "venus context N (NAME) ended while the guest closed
it: not a lost context" and counts nothing. The once-per-context guard of the ring-fatal
path is atomic (a context can have several rings).
Why: QEMU's GPU status counted lost virgl (GL) contexts only. A Venus context ended by
vkr's checks (ADR 0065) or the budget left lost at 0 (gpu_lost_delta 0 in all 4 lose
rows of the ghostty-gpu lane, 3.2.15), so OmacVM.app said nothing, omacvm check did not
name the app, and a Zink Ghostty that lost its context hung with no note.
Threading: virgl_gpu_budget_venus_lost runs on ring threads (any thread): a pthread
mutex around eight slots and an atomic count, which the renderer thread reads without
the lock first. closing and budget_refused are atomics; closing is stored with a full
fence before the fatal flag, and the ring thread fences after it saw the flag, before
it reads closing.
Upstream: not sent (the status file and the budget are OmacVM's).
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-venus-lost-count.c (build time, the public API, GL on Apple's
software renderer, Venus with a stub libvulkan): a bad ring command moves lost 0 -> 1
with the name, venus and kind venus, a second ring of the same app after it keeps 1; a
60 MB blob refused by a 64 MB budget, then a bad ring command: why guard. Without this
patch lost stays 0. Tests/virgl/test-venus-robust.c l-lost and l-teardown (the runtime's
real driver): a reply being written when its stream is closed, then a ring command:
"ended while the guest closed it", not "is lost" (before: "is lost").
Tests/virgl/test-resource-budget.c status: GL losses are kind gl.
